@page
@model WoodruffDev.Pages.Services.DevRelModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Developer Relations Services";
}

<div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
        <span class="section-label">Service</span>
        <h1>Developer Relations Services</h1>
        <p>Turn your platform, SDK, or API into something developers actually want to use&mdash;and talk about.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="service-detail fade-in">
            <h2>Service Overview</h2>
            <p>Great developer products aren&rsquo;t won on features alone. They&rsquo;re won on trust&mdash;earned through clear docs, credible voices, honest content, and a community that feels heard. I help companies design and run Developer Relations programs that move the metrics that actually matter: time-to-first-call, activation, retention, and developer sentiment.</p>
            <p>I&rsquo;ve built DevRel from the inside: leading developer relations at Rocket, serving as a Developer Advocate at JetBrains, speaking at conferences worldwide, hosting long-running podcasts, and earning Microsoft MVP status for .NET and Web Development. That mix of hands-on architecture and community work means I can shape a program that doesn&rsquo;t just look good on a slide&mdash;it ships.</p>

            <h2>Signals You Need This</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Your SDK, API, or platform has users but adoption is flat or churning silently</li>
                <li>You&rsquo;re hiring your first Developer Advocate and don&rsquo;t know how to scope the role</li>
                <li>Content output is inconsistent&mdash;blog posts, videos, and talks aren&rsquo;t reinforcing each other</li>
                <li>You invest in conferences and events but can&rsquo;t tell leadership what you got for it</li>
                <li>Internal engineers struggle with your own platform and there&rsquo;s no playbook to fix it</li>
                <li>A competitor&rsquo;s docs, samples, or community feel sharper than yours&mdash;and it&rsquo;s showing up in deals</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>What&rsquo;s Included</h2>
            <p>Engagements are modular&mdash;pick the pieces you need, or run the full program.</p>

            <h3>Developer Experience (DX) Audit</h3>
            <p>A structured review of onboarding, docs, samples, and time-to-first-success. You get a prioritized list of friction points and fixes, ranked by effort and impact.</p>

            <h3>Content Strategy &amp; Production</h3>
            <p>A documented content engine&mdash;editorial calendar, author network, publishing rhythm, distribution plan. I can ghost-write, co-write, or coach your team on technical posts, video scripts, reference applications, and launch narratives.</p>

            <h3>Conference &amp; Workshop Program</h3>
            <p>Talk selection, CFP strategy, speaker coaching, workshop curriculum design, and booth-to-followup playbooks. Built on 25+ years of speaking at international events.</p>

            <h3>Community Building</h3>
            <p>Forum / Discord / Slack strategy, office hours cadence, champions and ambassador programs, feedback loops to product. The goal: a community that reduces support load instead of amplifying it.</p>

            <h3>DevRel Team Design &amp; Hiring</h3>
            <p>Role scoping, ideal-candidate profiles, interview rubrics, compensation benchmarking, and onboarding plans. Also: OKRs and metrics that survive board-level scrutiny.</p>

            <h3>Internal Developer Enablement</h3>
            <p>Sales engineer training, internal docs, demo environments, and battlecards&mdash;so your go-to-market team can speak credibly to the developers they&rsquo;re selling to.</p>

            <h2>What You&rsquo;ll Walk Away With</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>A written DevRel strategy tied to business outcomes (acquisition, activation, retention)</li>
                <li>Baseline + target DX metrics (time-to-first-call, docs NPS, sample coverage, event ROI)</li>
                <li>A content pipeline with named owners and a publishing cadence</li>
                <li>A hiring playbook for your first (or next) DevRel hire</li>
                <li>Improvements in developer sentiment your team can measure</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Engagement Models</h2>
            <p><strong>Project</strong> &ndash; Scoped 3&ndash;6 month engagements (e.g., DX audit + launch content plan).</p>
            <p><strong>Fractional DevRel Lead</strong> &ndash; Embedded as your senior DevRel voice while you build the internal team.</p>
            <p><strong>Advisory</strong> &ndash; Quarterly or monthly guidance for an in-house DevRel leader who wants a seasoned sounding board.</p>

            <h2>Why Work With Me</h2>
            <p><strong>Both Sides of the House</strong> &ndash; I&rsquo;ve been the architect developers complain about and the advocate they turn to. That duality keeps the work honest.</p>
            <p><strong>Credibility on Stage and on Page</strong> &ndash; Conference keynotes, book author, podcast host, and MVP. Your program inherits reach, not just process.</p>
            <p><strong>Outcomes Over Optics</strong> &ndash; No vanity metrics. Every engagement ties to a number your CFO would sign off on.</p>

            <div class="service-cta">
                <h3>Ready to build something developers brag about?</h3>
                <p>Let&rsquo;s talk about your platform, your audience, and what&rsquo;s in the way.</p>
                <a href="/Contact" class="btn btn-primary">Start the Conversation <span class="btn-arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
            </div>

            <div class="service-nav">
                <a href="/Services/ProjectBased">&larr; Project-Based Contracts</a>
                <a href="/Services/Advisory">Advisory &amp; Board Roles &rarr;</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
